===Etymology 4===
From {{m|en|ring||algebraic structure}}, omitting the letter {{m|en|n}} to suggest the lack of [[negative]]s. Compare {{m|en|rng||structure like a ring but lacking a multiplicative identity}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|algebra|ring theory}} An [[algebraic structure]] similar to a [[ring]], but without the requirement that every element have an [[additive inverse]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2004|journal={{w|SIGPLAN|ACM SIGPLAN Notices}}|volume=39|publisher={{w|Association for Computing Machinery|ACM Press}}|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=LiJVAAAAMAAJ&q=%22rig,+but+not+a+ring%22&dq=%22rig,+but+not+a+ring%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwj2s4jg1araAhVHOrwKHWkuBvoQ6AEIJzAA|page=81
|passage=The set of natural numbers N with the usual operations of addition and multiplication is a '''rig''', but not a ring. The set of integers '''Z''' is a ring. For a '''rig'''/ring (R,0,+,1,−), the set of polynomials R[''x''] on a generator ''x'' with the usual operations of addition and multiplication is also a '''rig'''/ring.}}

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|algebraic structure like a ring but without additive inverses}} {{l|en|semiring}}

